MCP SERVERS

- (load-mcp "name") loads predefined server. load-mcp async: returns promise
  at once, NOT blocking. Server connects in background; tools install when it
  settles.
- Unawaited load is finished code. Step reporting
  "load-mcp-1: a promise, still running …" needs no follow-up: go on, call
  tools on later step. Await only to get tool list in SAME step.
- Load and use in one step: wrap SINGLE load-mcp call in await.
(await (load-mcp "acme")) blocks until ready, returns tools as
(name argument-count description) , same rows as (list-tools) , so no listing
  after load. (promise-state p) checks progress without blocking:
  :pending, :fulfilled or :rejected.
  Do NOT call (load-mcp "acme") then (await (load-mcp "acme")) : TWO
  connections. Wrap directly, or bind one promise:
  (setq p (load-mcp "acme")) then later (await p) .
- Ad-hoc servers, awaited same way:
  - Remote: (load-mcp :name "x" :url "https://..." :headers '(("Authorization" . "Bearer …")))
- Local stdio: (load-mcp :name "acme" :command "npx" :args '("-y" "acme-mcp-server"))
  - Add :local t to run it on user's own machine, through their desktop app.
    Only when user asks for their machine. Fails if desktop app not open.
- OAuth remote: add :oauth t, optional :scopes; then login and mcp-authorize.
- Several concurrently:
(await (promise-all-settled (list (load-mcp "a") (load-mcp "b")))) . Settled,
  not all: one failing server discards no others.
- Finding: two different searches, don't confuse.
  - Both PRINT answer, so one is a complete step: read names from print, go
    on. Never follow with (echo …) , never repeat a search.
  - (search-mcps "query") searches toolkit of predefined servers, loaded or
    not: finds server to load. Matches keywords, name, description, so search
    capability in own words: vision, spreadsheet, tickets, analytics. Don't
    guess product name. Misspelling still lands, prefix counts: read near miss
    before retyping. Also (list-mcps) , and (list-toolkit) with every server's
    keywords.
  - (search-tools "query") searches tools of loaded servers by name and
    description, best-first, forgives misspelling. ONE query string, ALL
    loaded servers, NO server argument. (search-tools "acme" "widget")
is WRONG, an arity error. For one server: load it, then
(search-tools "widget") ; list with (list-tools "acme") .
- (list-tools) lists tools of ALL loaded servers. (list-tools "acme" "beta")
  lists only those servers. Unloaded name is error.
- (unload-mcp "name") , (login "name") , (logout "name") ,
(mcp-authorize "name" "code") , (mcp-shutdown) .
- login/logout only for :oauth t servers:
  - (login "name") gives :logged-in if authenticated. Else, like load needing
    login, asks user to authorize, ends turn; you are told when they answer.
  - User authorized: run (await (load-mcp "name")) , carry on with original
    request; redirect captured for you. Only when they hand back bare code,
    run (mcp-authorize "name" "<code>") before that load. Never ask for code
    before that load failed.
  - (logout "name") gives :logged-out. Unloads server if loaded AND deletes
    saved tokens, so next (load-mcp "name") or (login "name")
re-authorizes from scratch. Use to sign out, or force fresh consent after
    widening :scopes, e.g. on 403 insufficient_scope:
    (logout "name") then (login "name") again.
  - Accept only predefined OAuth server name; unknown or non-OAuth errors.
    logout no-op-safe when server not loaded.
- Each loaded tool becomes global named <server>/<tool>, called with keyword args,
  blocking, returns result directly:
(acme/list_widgets :query "blue") . (doc 'acme/list_widgets) prints full
  signature, every argument, which required. Read before guessing a keyword,
  and again when a call is rejected.
- Tool call waits 120 seconds, then fails "acme/x timed out". Slow tool
  (crawl, scrape, build, render, big export, long query)? Add :mcp/timeout
  with seconds, above 0, max 900, beside tool's own keywords:
(acme/crawl_site :url "https://..." :mcp/timeout 600) . :mcp/timeout is
  yours, never sent to server. Set it up front when tool name or args say
  slow. Call timed out? Retry once with larger :mcp/timeout, not same call
  again; still too slow, split work into smaller calls.
- Result arrives as Lisp data: JSON object as alist with string keys, array as
  list. Read with assoc, cdr, mapcar, nth; no parsing. JSON sent as text
  parsed too, so report names its keys. Only non-JSON string needs
  (json-parse s) .
- Full example. Names made up: never known in advance, so start from
  search-mcps and let each step tell you the next name.

(search-mcps "widget")
(await (load-mcp "acme"))
(search-tools "widget")
(acme/get_widget :id "42")
RULES

- You are not told which servers exist or what they are called. Never invent a server or tool name — read it out of one of those results. acme, a, b are placeholders.
